{
  "_meta": {
    "purpose": "Frozen numbers. Generation reads this and never parses SRD text.",
    "method": "HP and DPR: corpus median where n>=8, mean of corpus and Blog of Holding formula where n=3-7, formula where n<3. Those two vary ~13%+ within a single CR, so a thin sample is noise. AC / attack bonus / save DC / per-attack damage: corpus median at any n, because those vary only 7-10% within a CR.",
    "attribution": "SRD 5.2.1 (WotC, CC BY 4.0); DPR/HP formulas from Blog of Holding (CC BY 4.0).",
    "beasts_excluded": "the 89 Beasts skew low-CR medians and are excluded",
    "smoothing": "AC / attack bonus / save DC / per-attack damage come from the corpus at any CR holding at least one non-Beast creature, however thin, because both the DMG table and the Blog of Holding formulas run far below the real high-CR monsters: the DMG table gives CR 24 AC 19 and attack +11, while both Ancient Dragons at that CR are AC 22 and +17. Empty CRs (18 and 25-29) interpolate linearly between the nearest populated CR on each side. NEVER flat-extrapolate from the last well-sampled row: that silently flattened CR 18-30 in an earlier attempt and every test still passed.",
    "per_attack_damage": "Signature attack, riders included: the Ancient Red Dragon's Rend is 19 Slashing plus 10 Fire, so 29. This field varies 31% within a single CR (AC varies 9%), because designers give high-CR creatures more attacks rather than harder ones, so it is pooled across neighbouring CRs until at least 8 creatures are in the sample. It is deliberately NOT forced to rise with CR: the real medians fall from 26 at CR 11 to 20 at CR 17. An earlier pass did clamp it monotonic, which smeared one Balor's 43-point Lightning Blade across every CR from 20 to 30.",
    "clamping": "AC, attack bonus and save DC are forced non-decreasing, because a generated CR 12 monster must not come out weaker than a CR 11 one. Where the clamp raised a row above its own corpus median, the row carries a `clamped` block naming the observed value and why. The usual cause is a CR holding only spellcasters, whose best attack roll is low because their damage comes from spells: CR 12 is only the Archmage (+9) and Erinyes (+8), while CR 11 fields the Behir (+10), Remorhaz (+11) and Roc (+13).",
    "pb": "PB = 2 + floor((CR-1)/4), the DMG rule. Verified against the corpus: CR 17 -> +6, CR 24 -> +7, CR 30 -> +9.",
    "initiative": {
      "rule": "Printed Initiative = DEX mod + delta_by_band. Measured 2026-08-03 across all 329 blocks: below CR 8 only 45/258 stamp anything above the raw DEX mod (delta 0); at CR 8-14 36/42 stamp, modal delta +4; at CR 15+ 29/29 stamp, modal delta +4 (the outliers are expertise-style +10..+18 on Solar-class blocks, not adopted). A CR 20 acting on raw DEX -2 is off-print (live: the Groan, Initiative -2 at the tier where parties alpha-strike).",
      "delta_by_band": { "0-7": 0, "8-14": 4, "15-20": 4, "21-30": 4 }
    },
    "dpr_target": "The AGGREGATE round: routine attacks + one area effect + two legendary uses. Measured against the corpus the aggregate lands at 1.00 / 0.99 / 0.95 of this figure for CR 0-7 / 8-14 / 15-30. The multiattack routine ALONE is only 0.86 / 0.72 / 0.47 of it, because high-CR creatures move their damage into area and legendary actions. Comparing a routine against this number will always look catastrophically low at high CR, and that is the comparison being wrong, not the creature.",
    "routine_share_by_band": {
      "0-7": 0.86,
      "8-14": 0.72,
      "15-30": 0.47
    },
    "dpr_target_with_area": "A creature carrying a gated area effect legitimately EXCEEDS dpr_target: published blocks run 1.57x at CR 0-7, 1.39x at CR 8-14 and 1.08x at CR 15-30, and the dragon wyrmlings reach 2.0-2.56x. The earlier 1.00x aggregate figure was a median over a population that mostly has no area at all, so it described creatures without one. Do not clamp a generated block back to dpr_target because it has an area: the recharge gate is what pays for the excess, and clamping would make every breath weapon weak."
  },
